---
.title = "Agent control",
.description = "Drive a running dev build from a coding agent or a script: read what a window shows, click, type, take screenshots and read logs, from the CLI or as MCP tools, without touching the desktop.",
.date = .date("2026-11-09T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Oriel"],
.layout = "docs.shtml",
---

Development builds (`oriel dev`) answer on a control socket of their own.
`oriel agent` talks to it from the terminal, and `oriel agent mcp` offers the
same commands as MCP tools, so a coding agent can check its own UI changes:
read the window's content as an outline with a ref for every element, click
and type into it, take a screenshot of the window and read the page's
console and the app's log. Nothing goes through the mouse, the keyboard or
a screen capture: the window can be covered, on another workspace or on a
headless display, and the agent never types into the wrong window.

Use it to test an app while developing it. It isn't a remote-control
feature for users: production builds don't contain it, and `oriel package`
refuses to package a binary that does.

# [Availability]($heading.id('availability'))

| Platform | Agent | Screenshots |
|---|---|---|
| Linux | Development builds (`oriel dev`, `zig build build-dev`) | WebView windows (WebKitGTK's own snapshot); not `-Dnative_ui` yet |
| macOS | Development builds; not tested yet | Not yet |
| Windows | Not yet | |
| Android, iOS | Not yet | |

Nothing to turn on: every development build starts the agent, and no
production build has it.

# [Quick start]($heading.id('quick-start'))

```sh
oriel dev                         # in one terminal
oriel agent snapshot              # in another, in the app's directory
```

```
# My App
# http://localhost:5173/
- main [ref=e1]
  - heading "Notes" [level=1] [ref=e2]
  - textbox "Title" [ref=e3] value=""
  - button "Save" [disabled] [ref=e4]
```

```sh
oriel agent fill e3 "Groceries"
oriel agent click "role=button[name=Save]"
oriel agent wait "text=Saved"
oriel agent screenshot            # prints the PNG's path
oriel agent logs                  # the app's log and the page's console
```

For an agent, register the MCP server once:

```sh
claude mcp add oriel -- oriel agent mcp      # Claude Code
```

Other MCP clients take the same command: `oriel agent mcp`, on stdio.

# [How it works]($heading.id('how-it-works'))

>[]($block.attrs("mermaid"))
```
flowchart LR
  Agent["coding agent"] -->|"MCP on stdio"| MCP["oriel agent mcp"]
  Shell["terminal"] --> CLI["oriel agent"]
  MCP -->|"JSON lines"| Sock["dev build: control socket"]
  CLI -->|"JSON lines"| Sock
  Sock -->|"evalJs (runtime + call)"| Page["window's page"]
  Page -->|"oriel:agent:reply (IPC)"| Sock
  Sock -->|"WebKit snapshot"| PNG["PNG file"]
```

`internal.run` starts the agent (`src/core/agent.zig`) when the app's
`App.Config.app.dev` is set, a comptime fact: in a production build the
agent is never analyzed, so none of its code, strings or page script is in
the binary. It listens on a Unix socket, `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/oriel-agent/<app
id>.sock` (else `$TMPDIR/oriel-agent-<uid>/` or `/tmp/oriel-agent-<uid>/`),
in a directory only the user can open (0700, checked to be the user's own),
the socket itself 0600, and serves each connection on a thread of its own
(at most 8 at once).

Requests are one JSON object per line, `{"id": 1, "method": "snapshot",
"params": {"window": "main"}}`; each gets one line back, `{"id": 1,
"result": ...}` or `{"id": 1, "error": "..."}`.

Most methods run in the window's page. The agent evaluates
`src/core/agent_runtime.js` there (once per page load: it defines
`window.__oriel_agent`), then the call; the page answers through the
`oriel:agent:reply` IPC command, which only development builds handle, and
the agent's thread waits for it (10 s by default). Scripts reach only the
app's own pages (the pages that get events), like `App.emit`. The page's
console is recorded from the agent's first call in that page; the app's own
log comes from `oriel.log.logFn` (the newest 1000 lines).

Actions are DOM events sent inside the page: a click is pointer and mouse
events at the element's centre (on what is really there: an overlay that
covers the element gets them, and the reply says so), then `click`; `fill`
sets the value through the element's own setter and sends `input` and
`change`, which React and the other frameworks see.

Screenshots come from the web view (`webkit_web_view_get_snapshot`, on the
main thread), written as PNG by GDK.

The CLI finds the app by its id: `--app`, else the `.id` in the project's
`build.zig`, else the only dev build running.

## [Packaging never ships it]($heading.id('packaging'))

Every package (`zig build package`, each format) takes its executables
through `package_tool check-no-agent`, which copies a binary only when it
doesn't contain the agent's marker (`src/core/agent_marker.zig`) and fails
the build otherwise:

```
error: zig-out/bin/my-app-dev contains Oriel's dev agent (a control socket for `oriel agent`):
       it is a development build and must not be packaged.
```

# [Examples]($heading.id('examples'))

## [Check a change, then look at it]($heading.id('check-a-change'))

```sh
oriel agent snapshot --interactive        # only what can be acted on
oriel agent fill "#email" "ada@example.com"
oriel agent press Enter --target "#email"
oriel agent wait ".toast" && oriel agent text ".toast"
oriel agent screenshot --out /tmp/after.png --full
```

## [Call a command, read the page's state]($heading.id('commands-and-eval'))

```sh
oriel agent invoke greet '{"name":"Ada"}'
oriel agent eval "document.querySelectorAll('li').length"
oriel agent eval "window.oriel.invoke('app_info')"   # promises are awaited
```

## [Follow the logs]($heading.id('follow-logs'))

```sh
oriel agent logs                 # ends with: --since 42 for newer lines
oriel agent logs --since 42 --page
```

## [A second window]($heading.id('second-window'))

```sh
oriel agent windows
oriel agent snapshot --window settings
```

## [Raw requests]($heading.id('raw-requests'))

```sh
oriel agent call wait_for '{"target":"text=Ready","timeout_ms":20000}'
```

```python
import json, os, socket
s = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
s.connect(os.environ["XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"] + "/oriel-agent/dev.oriel.MyApp.sock")
f = s.makefile("rw")
f.write(json.dumps({"id": 1, "method": "snapshot", "params": {}}) + "\n"); f.flush()
print(json.loads(f.readline())["result"]["snapshot"])
```

# [Reference]($heading.id('reference'))

## [Targets]($heading.id('targets'))

| Form | Finds |
|---|---|
| `e12` | The element with that ref in the last snapshot of the page (stale after a reload or once removed: take a new snapshot) |
| `text=Save` | The smallest visible element whose text is, or contains, "Save" (case-insensitive) |
| `role=button[name=Save]` | The first visible element with that role whose name contains "Save"; `role=dialog` alone: any |
| anything else | A CSS selector (`document.querySelector`) |

## [Snapshot lines]($heading.id('snapshot-lines'))

`- <role> "<name>" [<states>] [ref=eN] value="..." href="..."`, indented
under their container. Roles are ARIA roles, explicit (`role=`) or implied
by the element (`button`, `link`, `textbox`, `checkbox`, `combobox`,
`heading`, `list`, `listitem`, `dialog`, ...). States: `disabled`,
`checked`/`unchecked`, `expanded`/`collapsed`, `selected`, `current`,
`focused`, `level=N`. Text outside named elements appears as `text: "..."`.
Hidden elements (`display: none`, `visibility: hidden`, `aria-hidden`,
`hidden`) are left out. Output stops at 60,000 characters (pass a selector
to narrow it).

## [Methods]($heading.id('methods'))

Every method takes `window` (a label, default `"main"`); page methods also
take `timeout_ms` (default 10,000, at most 120,000).

| Method | Params | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `ping` | | `{app, pid, platform, renderer, agent}` |
| `windows` | | `[{label, title, width, height}]` |
| `info` | | `{url, title, readyState, size: {width, height, dpr}, focused}` |
| `snapshot` | `interactive?`, `selector?` | `{url, title, snapshot}` |
| `text` | `target?` | `{text}` |
| `click` | `target`, `double?` | `{clicked, covered}` (`covered`: what the click really hit, when not the target) |
| `hover` | `target` | `{hovered}` |
| `fill` | `target`, `value` | `{filled, value}` or `{filled, checked}` |
| `type` | `text`, `target?` (default: the focused element) | `{typed, value}` |
| `press` | `key` (`Enter`, `Control+a`, ...), `target?` | `{pressed, on, defaultPrevented}` |
| `scroll` | `target?`, `dx?`, `dy?` | `{scrollX, scrollY}` |
| `wait_for` | `target`, `state?` (`"visible"`, `"hidden"`), `timeout_ms?` (default 5,000, at most 60,000) | `{waited_ms}` |
| `eval` | `expression` | Its value as JSON (elements as `role "name"`; promises awaited) |
| `invoke` | `cmd`, `args?` | The command's result |
| `reload` | | `{reloading}` |
| `logs` | `since?` | `{entries: [{seq, level, text, time}], last}` (the page's console) |
| `app_logs` | `since?` | `{entries: [{seq, text}], last}` (the app's log) |
| `screenshot` | `path?` (absolute), `full_page?` | `{path, width, height}`; default path: in the socket directory |
| `quit` | | `true` |

`press` does what the browser would for the keys agents use most when the
page doesn't prevent it: Enter submits a form's input or clicks a button
or link, Space clicks a button, Tab moves the focus, Backspace deletes,
Control/Meta+a selects a field, printable keys insert.

## [oriel agent]($heading.id('cli'))

| Command | Method |
|---|---|
| `snapshot [--interactive] [--selector <css>]` | `snapshot` |
| `text [<target>]` | `text` |
| `click <target> [--double]`, `hover <target>` | `click`, `hover` |
| `fill <target> <value>` | `fill` |
| `type <text> [--target <t>]`, `press <key> [--target <t>]` | `type`, `press` |
| `scroll [--target <t>] [--dx N] [--dy N]` | `scroll` |
| `wait <target> [--hidden] [--timeout <ms>]` | `wait_for` |
| `eval <expression>`, `invoke <cmd> [<json>]` | `eval`, `invoke` |
| `screenshot [--out <file>] [--full]` | `screenshot` (prints the path) |
| `logs [--since N] [--page \| --app-log]` | `app_logs` and `logs` |
| `windows`, `info`, `reload`, `quit` | the same |
| `apps` | The running dev builds this user can drive |
| `call <method> [<json params>]` | Any method |
| `mcp` | The MCP server |

Every command takes `--app <id>` and `--window <label>`. Errors go to
stderr with exit code 1; wrong arguments exit with 2.

## [MCP tools]($heading.id('mcp-tools'))

`oriel agent mcp` serves MCP (protocol 2024-11-05 to 2025-06-18) on stdio
with the tools `oriel_apps`, `oriel_snapshot`, `oriel_click`, `oriel_fill`,
`oriel_type`, `oriel_press`, `oriel_hover`, `oriel_scroll`,
`oriel_wait_for`, `oriel_text`, `oriel_screenshot` (returns the image),
`oriel_logs` (`source`: `both`, `page`, `app`), `oriel_eval`,
`oriel_invoke`, `oriel_windows`, `oriel_info` and `oriel_reload`. Each
takes the method's params plus `app` and `window`. A failed call is a
result with `isError: true` and the reason. It connects for every call, so
it keeps working across the restarts `oriel dev` does after a Zig change.

# [Troubleshooting]($heading.id('troubleshooting'))

| Symptom | Cause and fix |
|---|---|
| `no dev build is running` | Start it with `oriel dev`; production builds (`oriel run`) have no agent. The app logs `dev agent on <path>` when it starts. |
| `several dev builds are running` | Pass `--app <id>` (`oriel agent apps` lists them). |
| `no answer within 10000 ms` | The window shows a page that isn't the app's own (an external site), or it is still loading: `oriel agent info`, then `wait`. |
| `ref e12 is stale` | The page changed or reloaded: take a new snapshot. |
| `dev agent off: cannot listen ... AnotherInstanceListens` | Another copy of the same dev build runs; the second one has no agent. |
| `screenshot isn't supported` | Not yet on that platform or with `-Dnative_ui`; `snapshot` and `text` work. |
| `contains Oriel's dev agent` while packaging | A dev executable reached the package (for example through `.package.contents.executables`); package the production one. |

